# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Azure DevOps — READ-ONLY configuration
#
# Copy this file to `.env` in the project root and fill in real values:
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored and must NEVER be committed.
# This file contains placeholders only — never put real values here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Full organization URL.
#   Example: https://dev.azure.com/my-organization
#   Legacy:  https://my-organization.visualstudio.com
ADO_ORG_URL=

# Azure DevOps project name (or project ID).
#   Example: NBO
ADO_PROJECT=

# Personal Access Token — READ-ONLY.
#
#   Required scope : Work Items (Read)
#   Create at      : <ADO_ORG_URL>/_usersSettings/tokens
#
# Grant the minimum scope needed. Do not reuse a full-access token.
# PATs expire (max 1 year, often less by org policy) — regenerate when rejected.
ADO_PAT_READ=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Personal Access Token — WRITE.
#
#   Required scope : Work Items (Read & write)
#   NOT required   : "Read, write & manage" — that adds destroy/permissions
#                    capability this project never uses. Do not grant it.
#   Create at      : <ADO_ORG_URL>/_usersSettings/tokens
#
# A SEPARATE token from ADO_PAT_READ. Never reuse the read token here, and never
# put this value in ADO_PAT_READ: keeping the two apart is what makes the read
# path structurally incapable of modifying Azure DevOps.
#
# Required only by the publisher (`npm run testcases:publish`). Every other
# command works without it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ADO_PAT_WRITE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional tuning (defaults shown)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADO_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# ADO_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Active project
#
# Which project under docs/projects/<KEY>/ a command acts on, when it is not
# passed explicitly as --project <KEY>.
#
# NOT a secret: this is a directory name, and it is printed in command output so
# a run always states which project it acted on.
#
# Optional. Required in practice only when MORE THAN ONE project profile exists
# and you do not want to pass --project on every command. With exactly one
# project, it may be left unset. When several exist and neither this variable nor
# --project is set, commands STOP and ask rather than guessing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
QA_ACTIVE_PROJECT=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Application under test — manual execution and Playwright automation
#
# Read for automation ONLY by src/automation/config.ts (invariant 7).
# Which variables apply, and which labels are allowed, come from the ACTIVE
# project's docs/projects/<KEY>/profile.md — never from this file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Explicit environment label. This — never the hostname — decides the
# environment. It must be on the active profile's "Allowed Environments" list.
# PROD is refused unconditionally; no profile can enable it.
#   Example: STG
APP_ENV=

# Base URL: the variable is NAMED by the profile's "Automation Base URL
# Variable" setting, with {ENV} replaced by the label. For example a profile
# setting of APP_{ENV}_ADMIN_URL with APP_ENV=STG reads APP_STG_ADMIN_URL.
# Add that variable here with the real URL.

# Test-data handles: the profile declares handle NAMES; values live here only.
#   Account handle       <HANDLE>_USERNAME=  and  <HANDLE>_PASSWORD=
#   Single-value handle  <HANDLE>=
# An unset handle makes the test BLOCKED / TEST_DATA_UNAVAILABLE, never FAIL.
